    "evidence_text": "Box 2.\n\nThe Burden of Trachoma in Ayod County of Southern Sudan\n\nRecent surveys have shown trachoma to be a serious cause of blindness in Southern Sudan.\n\nNo statistically significant difference was observed in the prevalence of trachoma signs between genders.\n\nCONCLUSIONS Trachoma is an unnecessary public health problem in Ayod.\n\nPURPOSE Glaucoma is the leading cause of global irreversible blindness.\n\nPrevalence and Causes of Blindness and Low Vision in Southern Sudan \n\nBackground Blindness and low vision are thought to be common in southern Sudan.\n\nEconomic impact of primary open-angle glaucoma in Australia.\n\nRESULTS There were no trials that primarily assessed visual screening.\n\nVisual impairment in this age group can usually be reduced with treatment.\n\nThe epidemiology of trachoma in Eastern Equatoria and Upper Nile States, southern Sudan.\n\nHowever, its magnitude and geographical distribution are largely unknown.\n\nTrachoma grading was done using the WHO simplified grading system.   \n\n\nCONCLUSION Trachoma is a public health problem in all nine of the study sites surveyed.\n\nThere is urgent need to implement trachoma control interventions in trachoma endemic regions of southern Sudan.\n\nHuman and other faeces as breeding media of the trachoma vector Musca sorbens.",
